Description: Jog wheel nudge fixes (side ring never scratches, latency-independent bend)
 1. The jog side ring is always a pitch bend. The stock DDJ-400 mapping routes
    all three jog rotation messages (side 0x21, platter 0x22/0x23) to one
    handler that scratches whenever [ChannelN],scratch2_enable is set,
    regardless of which part of the wheel moved. That flag stays set for a
    while after the platter top is released -- scratchDisable() ramps the rate
    back to playback speed before clearing it, and every scratchTick()
    restarts the ramp -- so nudging the side during that window scratched the
    deck and kept the window open. jogTurn() now scratches only for the
    platter message and only while the touch sensor says the top is actually
    held (new PioneerDDJ400.jogTouched state), and a bend arriving while a
    stale scratch is still active clears it first so the nudge is not
    swallowed by scratch2.
 2. Nudge response no longer depends on the audio buffer setting. The jog
    smoothing window (Rotary moving average in RateControl) was a fixed count
    of 25 audio buffers, i.e. ~125 ms at a 5 ms desktop buffer but well over
    half a second at the Pi's buffer size, and it kept applying the nudge for
    that whole window after the hand stopped -- so every beatmatch
    overcorrected. The window is now a fixed ~60 ms of real time, and the jog
    sensitivity is normalised against a reference buffer duration so a detent
    shifts the track by the same amount at any latency. Both changes answer
    upstream FIXMEs in ratecontrol.cpp. The mapping's bendScale (the
    per-detent sensitivity knob) drops 0.8 -> 0.4 to match.
Author: Pioneered CI <pioneered-build@localhost>
Forwarded: no

